{
  "id": "ep030",
  "duration": 51.13,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.075,
      "text": "A single teaspoon of a neutron star weighs one billion tons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Neutron star illustrated.jpg",
        "credit": "Casey Reed - Penn State University",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutron_star_illustrated.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.075,
      "end": 8.745,
      "text": "That is roughly nine hundred times the weight of the Great Pyramid of Giza.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Giza-pyramids.JPG",
        "credit": "Robster1983 at English Wikipedia",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giza-pyramids.JPG"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.067,
          "end": 6.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.867,
          "end": 8.733,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 8.745,
      "end": 14.415,
      "text": "Neutron stars are the collapsed cores of supergiant stars that died in a supernova.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Heic0609.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration. Acknowledgement: Robert A. Fesen (Dartmouth College, USA) and James Long (ESA/Hubble)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heic0609.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 8.733,
          "end": 11.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 11.567,
          "end": 14.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 14.415,
      "end": 23.385,
      "text": "In 1967, graduate student Jocelyn Bell Burnell detected a radio pulse so rhythmic she nicknamed it Little Green Men.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Jocelyn Bell Burnell.jpg",
        "credit": "Harry the Dirty Dog at English Wikipedia",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jocelyn_Bell_Burnell.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 14.4,
          "end": 23.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.385,
      "end": 29.005,
      "text": "These remnants pack the mass of a sun into a city-sized sphere only 12 miles wide.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Degenerate star sizes.png",
        "credit": "Grufo",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Degenerate_star_sizes.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.4,
          "end": 29.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 29.005,
      "end": 34.775,
      "text": "The star's gravity is so intense that the escape velocity exceeds half the speed of light.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Illustration of a gravitational lensing effect (heic0814f).tiff",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, and Johan Richard (Caltech, USA) Acknowledgement: Davide de Martin & James Long (ESA/Hubble)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustration_of_a_gravitational_lensing_effect_(heic0814f).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 29.0,
          "end": 34.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 34.775,
      "end": 43.35,
      "text": "In 2017, the LIGO observatory detected two of these giants colliding to forge the world's gold and platinum.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Eso1733s Artist's impression of merging neutron stars.jpg",
        "credit": "University of Warwick/Mark Garlick",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eso1733s_Artist%27s_impression_of_merging_neutron_stars.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 34.767,
          "end": 39.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 39.067,
          "end": 43.367,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.35,
      "end": 50.78,
      "text": "These cosmic impacts created the heavy metals found in our jewelry today, but their origin remains unimaginably dense.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Neutron star illustrated.jpg",
        "credit": "Casey Reed - Penn State University",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutron_star_illustrated.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.367,
          "end": 51.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
